revision.text:
{{Short description|Traditional English song}} {{Redirect|Here We Come A-Caroling|the Ray Conniff album|Here We Come A-Caroling (Ray Conniff album)}} {{use dmy dates|date=November 2024}} {{Listen |filename=U.S. Army Band - Here We Come A-wassailing.ogg |title="Here We Come A-wassailing" |description=Performed by the U.S. Army Band Chorus. }} "'''Here We Come A-wassailing'''" (or "Here We Come A-Caroling"), also known as "Here We Come A-Christmasing", "Wassail Song" and by many other names, is a traditional English [[Christmas carol]] and [[New Year]] song,<ref name="Hymnsandcarols">{{cite web|last=Anderson|first=Douglas D.|title=The Wassail Song|website=Hymnsandcarolsofchristmas.com|url=http://www.hymnsandcarolsofchristmas.com/Hymns_and_Carols/wassail_song-1.htm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190407231813/http://www.hymnsandcarolsofchristmas.com/Hymns_and_Carols/wassail_song-1.htm|url-status=dead|archive-date=2019-04-07}}</ref> typically sung whilst [[wassailing]], or singing carols, wishing good health and exchanging gifts door to door.<ref>[https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/wassail%5B2%5D "wassail"]. ''[[Merriam-Webster]] Online Dictionary''. 2008.</ref> It is listed as [[List of folk songs by Roud number|number 209]] in the [[Roud Folk Song Index]]. "[[Gower Wassail]]" and "[[Gloucestershire Wassail]]" are similar wassailing songs.
== History and context == The song dates from at least the mid 19th century,<ref>{{cite book|last=Husk|first=William Henry|author-link=William Henry Husk|title=Songs of the Nativity |date=1868 |publisher=J. C. Hotten |location=London |page=[https://archive.org/details/songsofnativityb00husk/page/152 152] |url=https://archive.org/details/songsofnativityb00husk|via=[[Internet Archive]]}}</ref> but is probably much older.<ref>{{cite book|chapter=Gloucestershire Wassail|title=[[The Oxford Book of Carols]]|year=1964|orig-year=1928|chapter-url=https://archive.org/details/oxfordbookofcaro0000perc_u3o4/page/32/mode/2up|pages=32–33|editor1=[[Percy Dearmer]]|editor2=[[Ralph Vaughan Williams]]|editor3=[[Martin Shaw (composer)|Martin Shaw]]|publisher=Oxford University Press|via=[[Internet Archive]]}}</ref><ref name="Hymnsandcarols" /> The ''[[wikt:a- -ing|a-]]'' in "a-wassailing" is an archaic intensifying prefix; compare "[[A-Hunting We Will Go]]" and lyrics to "[[The Twelve Days of Christmas (song)|The Twelve Days of Christmas]]" (e.g., "Six geese a-laying").
[[File:Wassail (4221293123).jpg|thumb|Wassail punch]] According to ''[[Reader's Digest]]'', "the Christmas spirit often made the rich a little more generous than usual, and bands of beggars and orphans used to dance their way through the snowy streets of England, offering to sing good cheer and to tell good fortune if the householder would give them a drink from his [[wassail]] bowl or a penny or a [[pork pie]] or, let them stand for a few minutes beside the warmth of his hearth. The wassail bowl itself was a hearty combination of hot ale or beer, apples, spices and mead, just alcoholic enough to warm tingling toes and fingers of the singers."<ref>[http://www.readersdigest.ca/features/heart/here-we-come-caroling "Here We Come A-Caroling"] ''[[Reader's Digest]]''</ref>
==Variants==
In 1949, the Welsh folk singer [[Phil Tanner]] sang a minor-key variant called "Wassail Song" and generally known as "[[Gower Wassail]]",<ref>{{Cite web|title=Wassail Song (Roud Folksong Index S193918)|url=https://www.vwml.org/record/RoudFS/S193918|access-date=2020-11-27|website=The Vaughan Williams Memorial Library}}</ref> which was popularised by various folk revival groups.
A variant is "Here We Come A-Christmasing". It replaces the word ''wassail'' with ''Christmas''.
There are also other variants (often, but not always, sung by Americans), wherein the first verse is sung "Here we come a-caroling" and it is titled so. Often in this version, the third verse (directly after the first refrain [see lyrics]) is removed, along with the refrain that follows it, but this depends on which version is being used. This version also often has the second line of the chorus "And a merry Christmas too" or "And to you glad tidings too", instead of "And to you your wassail too". There were other different satirical variants used on the 1987 Christmas special ''[[A Claymation Christmas Celebration]]''. Another variant is entitled "We've Been a While-A-Wandering" and "Yorkshire Wassail Song".
== Traditional collected versions == Hundreds of versions of wassailing songs have been collected, including dozens of variants collected by [[Cecil Sharp]] from the 1900s to the 1920s, mostly in the south of England.<ref>{{Cite web|last=|first=|date=|title=Search: rn209 cecil sharp|url=https://www.vwml.org/search?q=rn209%20cecil&is=1|access-date=|website=[[Vaughan Williams Memorial Library]]}}</ref> Many of the traditional versions that have been collected and recorded are not of the "Here We Come A-wassailing" variant; the following examples are similar to the now famous version:
* Emily Bishop of [[Bromsberrow|Bromsberrow Heath]], [[Gloucestershire]] (1952)<ref>{{Cite web|title=Wassail Song (Roud Folksong Index S224856)|url=https://www.vwml.org/record/RoudFS/S224856|access-date=2020-11-27|website=[[Vaughan Williams Memorial Library]]}}</ref> * Dorothy Davey of [[Kingston upon Hull|Hull]], [[Yorkshire]] (1969),<ref>{{Cite web|title=Here We Come A-wassailing (Roud Folksong Index S334844)|url=https://www.vwml.org/record/RoudFS/S334844|access-date=2020-11-27|website=[[Vaughan Williams Memorial Library]]}}</ref> available on the [[British Library Sound Archive]] website.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Here we come a wassailing – Steve Gardham English Folk Music Collection – World and traditional music|publisher=[[British Library]]|url=https://sounds.bl.uk/World-and-traditional-music/Steve-Gardham-Collection/025M-C1009X0005XX-5300V0|access-date=2020-11-27|website=sounds.bl.uk|archive-date=11 July 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170711050715/http://sounds.bl.uk/World-and-traditional-music/Steve-Gardham-Collection/025M-C1009X0005XX-5300V0|url-status=dead}}</ref> * George Dunn of [[Quarry Bank]], [[Staffordshire]] (1971), available online via the [[Vaughan Williams Memorial Library]]<ref>{{Cite web|title=Here We Come A-wasslin' (Roud Folksong Index S237732)|url=https://www.vwml.org/record/RoudFS/S237732|access-date=2020-11-27|website=[[Vaughan Williams Memorial Library]]}}</ref> * [[Frank Hinchliffe]] of [[Sheffield]], Yorkshire (1976)<ref>{{Cite web|title=We've Been a While A-wassailing (Roud Folksong Index S340466)|url=https://www.vwml.org/record/RoudFS/S340466|access-date=2020-11-27|website=[[Vaughan Williams Memorial Library]]}}</ref>
The song appears to have travelled to the United States with English settlers, where it has been found several times in the [[Appalachian Mountains|Appalachian]] region,<ref>{{Cite web|last=|first=|date=|title=Search: rn209 usa|url=https://www.vwml.org/search?q=rn209%20usa&is=1|access-date=|website=[[Vaughan Williams Memorial Library]]}}</ref> and recorded twice: * Edith Fitzpatrick James of [[Ashland, Kentucky]] (1934)<ref>{{Cite web|title=Wassail Song (Roud Folksong Index S265384)|url=https://www.vwml.org/record/RoudFS/S265384|access-date=2020-11-27|website=[[Vaughan Williams Memorial Library]]}}</ref> * [[Jean Ritchie]] of [[Viper, Kentucky]] (1949),<ref>{{Cite web|title=The Wassail Song (Roud Folksong Index S341721)|url=https://www.vwml.org/record/RoudFS/S341721|access-date=2020-11-27|website=[[Vaughan Williams Memorial Library]]}}</ref> available online as part of the [[Alan Lomax]] archive.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Alan Lomax Archive|url=http://research.culturalequity.org/get-audio-detailed-recording.do?recordingId=12730|access-date=2020-11-27|website=research.culturalequity.org|archive-date=2016-10-18|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161018165539/http://research.culturalequity.org/get-audio-detailed-recording.do?recordingId=12730|url-status=dead}}</ref>
== Lyrics == As with most carols, there are several related versions of the words. One version is presented below, based on the text given in ''[[The New Oxford Book of Carols]]''. The verses are sung in {{music|time|6|8}} time, while the chorus switches to common time.
<poem style="float:left; margin-left:1em;">1. Here we come a-wassailing Among the leaves so green; here we come a-wand'ring so fair to be seen.
{{0|—}}''Refrain:'' {{0|—}}Love and joy come to you, {{0|—}}and to you your wassail too; {{0|—}}and God bless you and send you a happy New Year, {{0|—}}and God send you a happy New Year.
2. Our wassail cup is made of the rosemary tree, and so is your beer of the best barley. {{0|—}}''Refrain''
3. We are not daily beggars that beg from door to door; but we are neighbours' children, whom you have seen before. {{0|—}}''Refrain''
4. Call up the butler of this house, put on his golden ring. Let him bring us up a glass of beer, and better we shall sing. {{0|—}}''Refrain''</poem> <poem style="margin-left:1em; float:left;">5. We have got a little purse of stretching leather skin; we want a little of your money to line it well within. {{0|—}}''Refrain''
{{break|3}}
6. Bring us out a table and spread it with a cloth; bring us out a mouldy cheese, and some of your Christmas loaf. {{0|—}}''Refrain''
7. God bless the master of this house likewise the mistress too, and all the little children that round the table go. {{0|—}}''Refrain''
8. Good master and good mistress, while you're sitting by the fire, pray think of us poor children who are wandering in the mire. {{0|—}}''Refrain''</poem>{{clear|left}}
